\section{Which assignment to launch}

Any FBF assignment of a Canvas course gives us a token, but the choice is
not free of consequences.
FBF creates the activities of a new course round lazily: when an assignment
copied from last year's course is launched for the first time, FBF copies
last year's activity into this year's FBF course.
A launch can therefore \emph{create} an activity, which we learned by doing
it.
That is harmless in itself, a teacher previewing the assignment does the
same, but a tool that only reads should not do it.
So we launch through an assignment whose activity surely exists: one with
submissions first, then a published one, which students may have opened.
Only if there is none do we take any FBF assignment.

<<functions>>=
def launch_order(assignments):
    """Return the FBF `assignments`, safest to launch first."""
    return sorted((a for a in assignments if placement_code(a)),
                  key=lambda a: (not getattr(a, "has_submitted_submissions",
                                             False),
                                 not getattr(a, "published", False)))
@

<<test functions>>=
def fbf_assignment(code, submitted=False, published=False):
  a = assignment(f"https://api.feedbackfruits.com/v1/lti/launch?code={code}")
  a.has_submitted_submissions = submitted
  a.published = published
  return a

def test_launch_order_prefers_submitted_then_published():
  draft = fbf_assignment("d")
  published = fbf_assignment("p", published=True)
  submitted = fbf_assignment("s", submitted=True, published=True)
  other = assignment("https://other.example.com/x?code=o")
  assert launch_order([draft, other, published, submitted]) \
    == [submitted, published, draft]
@

The client gets a [[relaunch]] that repeats the same launch, for when the
tokens can no longer be refreshed in a long run.

<<imports>>=
import functools
from fbf.api import FBF
from fbf.launch import launch
<<functions>>=
def connect(canvas_url, canvas_token, assignments):
    """Launch FBF through the safest of `assignments`; return an `FBF`.

    The `assignments` should be of one Canvas course; the client's
    `login.course_uuid` is then that course's FBF course.  Returns `None`
    if none of `assignments` is an FBF assignment.
    """
    for assignment in launch_order(assignments):
        relaunch = functools.partial(launch, canvas_url, canvas_token,
                                     assignment.course_id, assignment.id)
        return FBF(relaunch(), relaunch=relaunch)
    return None
@


\section{Naming assignments in the output}

The commands label their rows with the Canvas assignment name, which is how
the user selected them.
But names need not be unique: course 63874 has two FBF assignments with the
same name, and their rows could not be told apart.
We therefore add the assignment id to a name that repeats among the selected
assignments, and only then, since an id in every row would be noise in the
common case.
A name is compared exactly as Canvas shows it.

<<imports>>=
import collections
<<functions>>=
def assignment_labels(assignments):
    """Map assignment ids to labels: the name, plus the id if it repeats."""
    counts = collections.Counter(a.name for a in assignments)
    return {a.id: a.name if counts[a.name] == 1 else f"{a.name} ({a.id})"
            for a in assignments}
@

<<test functions>>=
def named(id, name):
  return SimpleNamespace(id=id, name=name)

def test_assignment_labels_add_the_id_only_to_repeated_names():
  labels = assignment_labels([named(1, "Week 1"), named(2, "Quiz"),
                              named(3, "Quiz")])
  assert labels == {1: "Week 1", 2: "Quiz (2)", 3: "Quiz (3)"}
