\section{Finding the activities of this round}

The [[code]] in a Canvas assignment's launch URL is not specific to the
course round.
When Canvas copies a course, the copied assignments keep the code, and FBF
resolves it in the context of the launch: the same code opens last year's
activity from last year's course and this year's from this year's.
A global lookup by code---[[/v1/lti/placements?filter[code]=...]]---finds
the placement of the round that created the code.
That was our first approach, and it listed discussions from three earlier
rounds mixed with this one's.

So we resolve codes within the FBF course of the launch, which is the course
of the Canvas course we launched from.
Its activities come with their placements ([[include=placement]]), and each
placement carries the code.
An assignment whose code is not among them has no activity in this round
yet: nobody has opened it.

<<imports>>=
from fbf.schema import attribute, related_id, requires
<<required names>>=
requires("lti/placement", attributes=["code"], relationships=["placeable"])
requires("activities", relationships=["placement"])
<<functions>>=
def activities_by_code(fbf, course_uuid):
    """Map placement codes to activity ids in the FBF course `course_uuid`."""
    document = fbf.get(f"v1/courses/{course_uuid}/activities",
                       include="placement")
    placements = {id: placement
                  for (kind, id), placement in document.included.items()
                  if kind == "lti/placement"}
    activities = {}
    for activity in document.resources:
        placement = placements.get(related_id(activity, "placement"))
        if placement:
            activities[attribute(placement, "code")] = activity["id"]
    return activities
@

Commands work on assignments, possibly of several Canvas courses.
[[fbf_activities]] does the launches and the lookups for them: one launch
per Canvas course, since each course has its own FBF course.
It yields each assignment with its client and activity, or with [[None]]
for an assignment that has no activity in this round, so that the command
can say so.

<<imports>>=
import itertools
<<functions>>=
def fbf_activities(canvas_url, canvas_token, assignments):
    """Yield `(assignment, fbf, activity_id)` for the FBF `assignments`.

    `activity_id` is `None` when the assignment has not been opened in FBF
    in its course round.
    """
    by_course = lambda a: a.course_id
    fbf_assignments = sorted((a for a in assignments if placement_code(a)),
                             key=by_course)
    for _, course in itertools.groupby(fbf_assignments, key=by_course):
        course = list(course)
        fbf = connect(canvas_url, canvas_token, course)
        codes = activities_by_code(fbf, fbf.login.course_uuid)
        for assignment in course:
            yield assignment, fbf, codes.get(placement_code(assignment))
@

The contract test of \cref{Schema} needs an example of a placement to check
the names above on.
The launched activity has one: its [[placement]] relationship refers to it,
and FBF includes it when asked.
<<example fetchers>>=
@example("lti/placement")
def placement_of_the_launched_activity(fbf, login):
    document = fbf.get(f"v1/activities/{login.activity_uuid}",
                       include="placement")
    return fbf.related(document.resource, "placement",
                       included=document.included)
@

The same placement lets us test [[activities_by_code]] against FBF.
The code of the launched activity's placement must lead back to that
activity among the activities of the launched course.
That holds on any day, live or replayed, and it fails if we read the
placements wrongly or look in the wrong course.
<<test functions>>=
import pytest

import conftest
from fbf.schema import attribute

@pytest.mark.vcr
def test_activities_by_code_finds_the_launched_activity(fbf):
  placement = conftest.EXAMPLES["lti/placement"](fbf, fbf.login)
  code = attribute(placement, "code")
  codes = activities_by_code(fbf, fbf.login.course_uuid)
  assert codes[code] == fbf.login.activity_uuid
